---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
const title = 'Security and Trust | WrenLore';
const description = 'How WrenLore approaches private deployment, identity, permissions, auditability, dependency maintenance and responsible vulnerability reporting.';
const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'WebPage',
  name: title,
  url: new URL('/security/', Astro.site),
  description,
  about: { '@type': 'SoftwareApplication', name: 'WrenLore' },
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/security/" schema={schema}>
  <Header />
  <main class="shell narrow legal-page">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">WrenLore</a><span>/</span>Security</p><h1>Security and trust</h1>
    <p class="lead">WrenLore stores operational knowledge and can expose bounded workflows to AI agents. That deserves a concrete security model, including the parts the operator still has to own.</p>
    <section><h2>Deployment boundary</h2><p>WrenLore runs on infrastructure controlled by the operator and does not require a mandatory WrenLore cloud control plane. The documented stack uses the application, PostgreSQL with pgvector, and Redis. Operators choose the network boundary, reverse proxy, TLS configuration, durable storage and backup location.</p><p>Self-hosting changes who controls the system. It does not harden the host automatically. Operating-system updates, container provenance, secrets, firewall policy, monitoring, log retention, backups and tested recovery remain deployment responsibilities.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Identity and access</h2><p>WrenLore supports local authentication and SAML configuration. Access is evaluated through workspace roles, spaces and page restrictions. Administrators should connect identity lifecycle to their existing process, remove stale accounts, restrict privileged roles and test access using representative private pages.</p><p>A SAML login is one part of the boundary. The security of the identity provider, session configuration, email service and administrator accounts still matters. WrenLore does not claim that enabling SSO creates compliance certification or removes the need for operational controls.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Agent and MCP access</h2><p>MCP connections are named and owned by a real user. A connection can be read-only or read/write, can be restricted to selected spaces, can expire, and can be revoked independently. WrenLore stores a hash of the bearer token. The current user, token scope and page permissions are checked when tools run.</p><p>Writes require an expected page revision and a request key, which reduces accidental duplicate edits and catches concurrent changes. Activity is attributed to the user via the named agent connection. MCP V1 deliberately omits deletion, moves, sharing, permission changes, history restoration and arbitrary attachment uploads.</p><p>This is still bearer-token authentication, not an OAuth authorization server. Tokens belong in a client’s secret store and should never be pasted into shared documentation, logs or screenshots.</p></section>
    <section><h2>AI and data movement</h2><p>Core wiki functions do not require an external AI service. When an operator configures a remote model provider, the content sent for inference crosses the WrenLore deployment boundary and is handled under that provider’s terms and controls. Teams with strict requirements should choose an approved endpoint, use a suitable local model path, or leave AI features disabled.</p><p>Permission-aware retrieval reduces accidental exposure inside WrenLore. It cannot compensate for an insecure model endpoint, a compromised administrator, poor network policy or sensitive material copied elsewhere by an authorised user.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Auditability and recovery</h2><p>Page revisions, activity history and user-via-agent attribution provide evidence about changes made through the application. They are useful for review and incident investigation. They are not a substitute for infrastructure logs, database backups or an independent recovery process.</p><p>Back up the PostgreSQL data and file storage together, protect backup credentials separately, and test restoration. A backup that has never been restored is an optimistic storage bill.</p></section>
    <section><h2>Dependency and release maintenance</h2><p>The project records public releases and security maintenance in GitHub. Operators should pin a reviewed release or commit, inspect release notes, test upgrades away from production, and keep the surrounding host and proxy stack patched. Public source makes inspection possible; it does not make every deployment secure by default.</p></section>
    <section><h2>What is not claimed</h2><ul><li>No published SOC 2, ISO 27001 or similar certification is claimed.</li><li>No managed security monitoring, hosted backup service or contractual response SLA is currently sold.</li><li>No blanket air-gap guarantee is made; isolated operation depends on the operator’s identity, mail, model, image and update design.</li><li>No software can promise safety when privileged accounts, hosts or connected clients are compromised.</li></ul></section>
    <section><h2>Report a vulnerability</h2><p>Do not open a public issue for an undisclosed vulnerability. Use GitHub’s private vulnerability reporting channel and include affected versions, reproduction details and impact where it is safe to do so.</p><a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/wrenlore/wrenlore/security">Open GitHub Security</a> <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/wrenlore/wrenlore/blob/master/docs/MCP.md">Review the MCP security model</a></section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
